            "A bleeding history asks <strong>where, how much, how long, since when, and who else</strong>. <strong>Platelet-type bleeding</strong> — from too few platelets, platelets that do not work (aspirin, NSAIDs, uraemia) or missing von Willebrand factor — is <strong>mucocutaneous and immediate</strong>: petechiae (pinpoint, non-blanching, on the shins, where the blood-pressure cuff was, on the palate), purpura and easy bruising, bleeding gums on brushing, nosebleeds, heavy periods from the first, prolonged oozing from cuts and venepuncture sites, bleeding into the gut and bladder. <strong>Factor-type bleeding</strong> — haemophilia, liver disease, warfarin, vitamin K deficiency — is <strong>deep and delayed</strong>: a cut stops and then starts again hours later; bleeding into <strong>joints</strong> (knees, elbows, ankles) and <strong>muscles</strong> (the thigh, the calf, the iliopsoas presenting as a flexed hip and a numb thigh), large spreading bruises, and bleeding into the brain. <strong>Disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong> shows both at once in a patient who is already very ill: oozing from every puncture site, bruising around cannulae, and organ failure.",
