            "<strong>Haemophilia A</strong> (factor VIII deficiency, about one in 5,000 boys) and <strong>haemophilia B</strong> (factor IX, about one in 30,000) are <strong>X-linked recessive</strong>: a carrier mother passes the gene to half her sons, who are affected, and half her daughters, who are carriers; a father cannot pass it to his son; the family history is of <strong>brothers and maternal uncles</strong>, though a third of cases are new mutations with no history at all. Carrier women can have low enough levels to bleed with surgery and childbirth. Severity follows the factor level: <strong>severe (under 1%)</strong> bleeds spontaneously into joints and muscles from the first year — in Ghana often announced by <strong>prolonged bleeding after circumcision</strong>, then bruising when the child starts to crawl and a swollen knee after a fall; <strong>moderate (1–5%)</strong> bleeds with minor injury; <strong>mild (5–40%)</strong> is found only at surgery, extraction or a serious accident. The laboratory shows a <strong>prolonged APTT with a normal PT and normal platelets</strong>, and the factor assay names the type and level. Repeated <strong>haemarthroses</strong> destroy the joint (a chronic swollen stiff knee by adolescence), <strong>muscle bleeds</strong> cause compartment syndrome and nerve damage, and <strong>intracranial haemorrhage</strong>, after a head bump that other children would shrug off, is the leading cause of death.",
            "Treatment is <strong>the missing factor, as early as possible</strong>: the boy who says his knee ‘feels funny’ is treated before it swells, because the first dose given within an hour saves the joint and the dose given the next morning does not. Factor VIII 1 unit/kg raises the level by about 2%; factor IX 1 unit/kg by about 1%; a joint bleed is treated to 30–50% and a head injury or major bleed to 100% — so a 20 kg boy with a knee bleed needs roughly 400–500 units of factor VIII, and <strong>a head injury is treated first and scanned second</strong>. Factor concentrate reaches Ghana mainly through the World Federation of Hemophilia humanitarian programme and the Ghana Haemophilia Society, held at the teaching hospital treatment centres; where no concentrate exists, <strong>cryoprecipitate</strong> (factor VIII, von Willebrand factor and fibrinogen) treats haemophilia A and <strong>fresh frozen plasma</strong> treats either, in volumes that limit what can be achieved. Around the factor: <strong>rest, ice, compression and elevation</strong> for a joint or muscle bleed; <strong>paracetamol</strong>, never aspirin or NSAIDs; <strong>no intramuscular injections</strong> (vaccines subcutaneously or with the finest needle and ten minutes of pressure); <strong>desmopressin</strong> for mild haemophilia A and von Willebrand disease (it releases the body's own stores; fluids are restricted afterwards because it causes water retention and dangerous hyponatraemia in small children); <strong>tranexamic acid</strong> for mouth, nose and dental bleeding (never with blood in the urine, where it clots the ureters); dental care so that extractions are rare and planned; physiotherapy to keep the muscles that protect the joints; a <strong>medical alert card</strong>; and teaching the family to recognise and treat bleeds at home. Some patients develop <strong>inhibitors</strong> — antibodies against the factor — and need bypassing agents; the generation treated with plasma before screening carries HIV and hepatitis C, which is why donated plasma is tested.",
